% @PACKAGE_NAME@(n) @PACKAGE_VERSION@ | Tcl Source Parser
% Cyan Ogilvie
% @PACKAGE_VERSION@


# NAME

@PACKAGE_NAME@ - Parse Tcl source into a tDOM tree


## SYNOPSIS

**package require @PACKAGE_NAME@** ?@PACKAGE_VERSION@?

**parsetcl** **parsetree** *script*\
**parsetcl** **ast** *script*\
**parsetcl** **subparse** *mode* *wordNode* ?*arg* ...?

**parsetcl::parse** *mode* *text* ?*ofs*? ?*ofsLine*?\
**parsetcl::xpath** *node* *xpath*\
**parsetcl::expr2stack** *exprTokens*

### Note
The package namespace is registered as an ensemble (with **-prefixes no**),
so the **parsetree**, **ast** and **subparse** subcommands are also
addressable as **parsetcl::parsetree**, **parsetcl::ast** and
**parsetcl::subparse**.  This package is in its 0 series and the API may
change in backwards-incompatible ways - see **STABILITY** below.


## DESCRIPTION

This package parses Tcl source text - a script, an expression, a list,
or a substitutable string - into a [tDOM](http://www.tdom.org/) tree
representing each token, its byte offset and length in the source, and
(for full parse trees) its line and character position.  The parsed tree
is suitable for syntax-aware tooling: highlighters, refactorers, linters,
formatters, indenters, code-graph builders, and any other tool that needs
to inspect Tcl source without executing it.

Two flavours of tree are produced:

**parsetree** (`<tcl>` root)
:   The full ("concrete") parse tree.  Preserves every byte of the input,
    including whitespace, comments, syntax characters (braces, quotes,
    brackets, dollars, backslashes), command terminators, and the `{*}`
    expansion marker.  Each script node carries a **linestarts** index
    so that any byte offset inside it can be cheaply mapped to a 1-based
    line and character position.  Use this when you need round-trip
    fidelity (formatters, refactorers, syntax highlighters).

**ast** (`<ast>` root)
:   A compact tree without line-tracking attributes.  Same node structure
    as **parsetree** otherwise.  Use this when you only need to walk the
    structural skeleton of the script.

Both forms are produced by a fast C parser and cached on the script
**Tcl\_Obj** as a custom **Tcl\_ObjType** (named `parsetree` and `ast`
respectively).  Calling **parsetree** or **ast** a second time on the
same unmodified script value is therefore essentially free - the cached
tree is returned without re-parsing.

The returned value is the **tDOM** node command for the document element
- you walk and query it with the usual **domNode** / **selectNodes**
methods, or with the **parsetcl::xpath** convenience wrapper.

> **Tree ownership:** the document is owned by the script **Tcl\_Obj**'s
> internal representation, **not** by the returned node command.  Do not
> call **delete** on the root node, and do not retain the node command
> past the lifetime of the script value - if the script value is freed
> or has its string representation invalidated, the node command becomes
> a dangling reference.

A second pass, **subparse**, descends into a literal-valued **word** node
in an existing tree and re-parses its content as a different language
(script, expression, list, or subst), attaching the result as an **as**
child.  This is how you parse the body of an **if** condition as an
expression, the body of a **proc** as a script, the keys of a **switch**
as a list, and so on.  The pass is driven from Tcl - this package does
not know which words of which commands should be re-parsed - and the
caller is expected to apply command-specific knowledge (for example,
"the second word of `if` is an expression").

### Performance model

The C parser is the fast path.  A typical small script (a few KiB)
parses in tens of microseconds and the result is cached on the
**Tcl\_Obj** for free re-use.  The Tcl-level **parsetcl::parse**
reference parser is two to three orders of magnitude slower and exists
mainly to validate the C implementation; prefer **parsetree** and
**ast** for any production use.


## COMMANDS

**parsetcl** **parsetree** *script*
:   Parse *script* as a Tcl script and return the **tDOM** node command
    for the root **&lt;tcl&gt;** element.  The tree contains an inner
    **&lt;script&gt;** element with one **&lt;command&gt;** child per
    command in the source, and **noise** tokens (whitespace, comments,
    syntax characters, command terminators, `{*}` expansion markers)
    are preserved as siblings of the **&lt;word&gt;** children.  See
    **TREE STRUCTURE** for the complete node and attribute reference.

    The result is cached as the **parsetree** **Tcl\_ObjType** on
    *script*; subsequent calls with the same value return the cached
    tree without re-parsing.

    Throws a **PARSETCL** **PARSE** *subcode* exception if *script* is
    not syntactically valid Tcl.  See **EXCEPTIONS** for the subcodes.

**parsetcl** **ast** *script*
:   Parse *script* and return the **tDOM** node command for the root
    **&lt;ast&gt;** element.  Structurally identical to the tree
    produced by **parsetree** (so the same node names, **xpath**
    expressions and walking code work against either), but cached
    independently as the **ast** **Tcl\_ObjType** on *script*.

**parsetcl** **subparse** *mode* *wordNode* ?*arg* ...?
:   Re-parse the literal value of *wordNode* (a **&lt;word&gt;** node
    from a tree built by **parsetree** or **ast**) as the language
    given by *mode* and attach the result as an **&lt;as&gt;** child of
    *wordNode*.  *wordNode* must be a node command for a **&lt;word&gt;**
    element that has a **value** attribute (i.e. its content was a
    static literal at parse time); words whose value depends on
    substitutions are silently skipped.

    *mode* selects the sub-grammar:

    **script**
    :   Parse the value as a Tcl script.  Produces a **&lt;script&gt;**
        subtree under **&lt;as&gt;**.

    **expr**
    :   Parse the value as a Tcl expression.  Produces an
        **&lt;expr&gt;** subtree containing nested **&lt;subexpr&gt;**
        and **&lt;operator&gt;** nodes.

    **list**
    :   Parse the value as a Tcl list.  Produces a **&lt;list&gt;**
        subtree of **&lt;word&gt;** children separated by
        **&lt;space&gt;** noise.

    **subst**
    :   Parse the value as the argument to a **subst** command.
        Produces a **&lt;subst&gt;** subtree containing **&lt;text&gt;**,
        **&lt;escape&gt;**, **&lt;var&gt;**, and **&lt;script&gt;**
        children.  The optional switches **-nocommands**, **-novariables**
        and **-nobackslashes** suppress the corresponding substitution
        forms (mirroring the flags accepted by Tcl's built-in **subst**).

    **sql**, **json**, **javascript**
    :   Reserved for future extension.  Currently these store the
        unparsed text in a corresponding **&lt;sql&gt;**,
        **&lt;json&gt;** or **&lt;javascript&gt;** node tagged with an
        empty **unparsed** attribute, without further analysis.

    Throws a **PARSETCL** **PARSE** *subcode* exception if the embedded
    text doesn't parse as the requested *mode*.

**parsetcl::parse** *mode* *text* ?*ofs*? ?*ofsLine*?
:   Tcl-level reference parser.  Same modes as **subparse** (excluding
    **sql**/**json**/**javascript**), but operates directly on a string
    rather than on a **&lt;word&gt;** node and returns the root tDOM
    node command for the new tree (the document is created as a side
    effect and is not cached on *text*).

    *ofs* (default 0) is added to all **idx** attributes; *ofsLine*
    (default 1) is added to all **line** attributes.  Use these when
    parsing a fragment whose position inside a larger source is known.

    This parser is **much slower** than **parsetree**/**ast** and is
    intended for testing, bootstrapping, and as an executable
    specification of the grammar.

**parsetcl::xpath** *node* *xpath*
:   Convenience wrapper for **\$node selectNodes \$xpath**.  Equivalent
    to writing **domNode \$node selectNodes \$xpath**, but shorter at
    the call site.

**parsetcl::expr2stack** *exprTokens*
:   Convert a flat JSON list of expression tokens (as produced by the
    Tcl-level expression parser) into a postfix / RPN representation
    using a shunting-yard algorithm.  Mainly useful as a building
    block for expression evaluators built on top of this package.


## TREE STRUCTURE

Every tree produced by **parsetree** or **ast** consists of nested
**tDOM** element nodes.  Element names indicate the token category;
attributes carry source-position metadata and (where derivable at parse
time) constant-folded values.

### Top-level wrapper

**&lt;tcl&gt;** (parsetree only) / **&lt;ast&gt;** (ast only)
:   Document element.  Contains exactly one **&lt;script&gt;** child.

### Structural nodes

**&lt;script&gt;**
:   A Tcl script.  Contains zero or more **&lt;command&gt;** children
    interleaved with noise (**&lt;space&gt;**, **&lt;comment&gt;**,
    **&lt;syntax&gt;**).  Carries **idx**, **len**, **lineofs** and
    **linestarts** attributes (see **ATTRIBUTES** below).

**&lt;command&gt;**
:   A single Tcl command.  Contains a sequence of **&lt;word&gt;**
    children separated by **&lt;space&gt;** noise, terminated by an
    **&lt;end&gt;** node carrying the literal terminator
    (`\n`, `;` or `]`) or empty for the synthetic terminator at end
    of input.  If the first word of the command is a static literal,
    the command node carries a **name** attribute with that literal
    value - the cheap way to dispatch on command name without walking
    children.

**&lt;word&gt;**
:   A single argument to a command (or list element, or function
    argument).  Carries a **quoted** attribute (**none**, **brace**
    or **quote**) describing how the word was delimited in the source.
    If the word's value is a static literal (no substitutions, no
    dynamic content), the word also carries a **value** attribute with
    the fully-substituted constant value - this is what **subparse**
    consumes.  An **expand** attribute is set on words prefixed with
    `{*}`.

    Children of **&lt;word&gt;** are the substitution tokens that make
    it up: **&lt;text&gt;**, **&lt;escape&gt;**, **&lt;var&gt;**,
    **&lt;array&gt;**, **&lt;script&gt;** (for **\[ ... \]** command
    substitution), and **&lt;syntax&gt;** noise for the surrounding
    quotes/braces.

### Substitution tokens (children of &lt;word&gt;)

**&lt;text&gt;**
:   Plain literal text.  Element text content is the literal characters
    from the source.

**&lt;escape&gt;**
:   A backslash escape (e.g. `\n`, `\u306f`, `\x65`, `\101`).  Element
    text content is the source representation; the **norm** attribute
    (when present) holds the normalized expansion.

**&lt;var&gt;**
:   A scalar variable substitution (`$foo`, `${name}`).  Carries
    **type="scalar"** and **name** attributes.

**&lt;array&gt;**
:   An array element substitution (`$arr(idx)`).  Same structure as
    **&lt;var&gt;** but with a child **&lt;index&gt;** node holding
    the index expression as a sub-tree of substitution tokens; if
    the index is a static literal the **&lt;index&gt;** node also
    carries a **value** attribute.

**&lt;script&gt;**
:   A nested command substitution (`[ ... ]`).  Same structure as a
    top-level script.

### Expression tokens (under &lt;expr&gt;)

**&lt;expr&gt;**
:   Root of an expression sub-tree (produced by **subparse expr** or
    **parse expr**).  Contains a single **&lt;subexpr&gt;** child.

**&lt;subexpr&gt;**
:   A sub-expression.  Carries **idx**, **len** and **orig**
    attributes; if it evaluates to a constant at parse time it also
    carries a **value** attribute.  Children are either further
    **&lt;subexpr&gt;** nodes joined by **&lt;operator&gt;** nodes,
    or substitution tokens (**&lt;text&gt;**, **&lt;var&gt;**,
    **&lt;script&gt;**, ...) for leaf operands.

**&lt;operator&gt;**
:   A binary, unary or ternary operator.  Carries a **name** attribute
    (`+`, `-`, `*`, `<`, `==`, `?`, ...) and contains its operand
    sub-expressions as children.

### Sub-grammar containers

**&lt;list&gt;**
:   Root of a list sub-tree (produced by **subparse list** or
    **parse list**).  Contains **&lt;word&gt;** children separated
    by **&lt;space&gt;** noise.

**&lt;subst&gt;**
:   Root of a subst sub-tree (produced by **subparse subst** or
    **parse subst**).  Contains a sequence of **&lt;text&gt;**,
    **&lt;escape&gt;**, **&lt;var&gt;** and **&lt;script&gt;**
    children.

**&lt;as&gt;**
:   Wrapper attached as a child of **&lt;word&gt;** by **subparse**.
    Holds one or more sub-trees representing alternative parses of
    the same word's value (e.g. as both a script and an expression).
    Look here when traversing a tree to find what **subparse** has
    decided about each word.

### Noise tokens

These nodes are present in **parsetree** output to preserve round-trip
fidelity but are tagged with an empty **noise** attribute so they can
be cheaply skipped in xpath queries (e.g.
`*[not(@noise)]`).

**&lt;space&gt;**
:   Whitespace - spaces, tabs and newlines that separate words or
    commands.  Element text content is the literal characters.

**&lt;comment&gt;**
:   A `#`-introduced comment.  Element text content is the comment
    line including the leading `#` and trailing newline.

**&lt;syntax&gt;**
:   A syntactic delimiter character: `{`, `}`, `"`, `[`, `]`,
    `$`, opening/closing parens of array indices, ...

**&lt;end&gt;**
:   A command terminator (`\n`, `;`, `]`, or empty for the synthetic
    terminator at end of input).

**&lt;expand&gt;**
:   The `{*}` word-expansion marker.


## ATTRIBUTES

**idx**
:   Byte offset of the token's first character in the original source
    text.  Always 0-based.  Present on every parsed node.

**len**
:   Length of the token in bytes (parsetree/ast output from the C
    parser uses **len**).  Present on every parsed node.

**length**
:   Length in bytes (Tcl-level **parse** output uses **length**
    instead of **len** for backwards compatibility with the original
    Tcl reference parser).

**line**, **char**
:   1-based line number and character position of the token's first
    character.  Set on every node by the Tcl-level **parse**
    reference parser; on **parsetree** output computed lazily from
    the per-script **linestarts** index when needed.

**lineofs**, **linestarts**
:   Set on **&lt;script&gt;** nodes in **parsetree** output.
    **lineofs** is the line number of the script's first line minus 1
    (so that adding **lineofs** to a within-script line number gives
    the absolute line); **linestarts** is a space-separated list of
    byte offsets at which lines begin within this script.  Together
    they let any byte offset inside the script be mapped to an
    absolute (line, char) pair without rescanning the source.

**name**
:   On **&lt;command&gt;**: the literal value of the first word, when
    that word is a static literal.  On **&lt;var&gt;** and
    **&lt;array&gt;**: the variable name.  On **&lt;operator&gt;**:
    the operator symbol.

**value**
:   On **&lt;word&gt;**, **&lt;index&gt;** or **&lt;subexpr&gt;**:
    the fully-resolved constant value, when the token's content is a
    static literal (no substitutions).  Absent when the value would
    depend on runtime substitutions.

**varvalue**
:   On **&lt;word&gt;**: the source-text form of a single-variable
    word (e.g. `foo` or `arr(idx)`), set when the word consists of
    exactly one variable substitution and nothing else.  Useful for
    cheap "is this word just `\$x`?" tests.

**quoted**
:   On **&lt;word&gt;**: how the word was delimited in the source -
    **none** (bareword), **brace** (`{...}`) or **quote** (`"..."`).

**expand**
:   On **&lt;word&gt;**: empty attribute set when the word was
    prefixed with `{*}`.

**type**
:   On **&lt;var&gt;** / **&lt;array&gt;**: **scalar** or **array**.

**noise**
:   Empty attribute set on every **&lt;space&gt;**, **&lt;comment&gt;**,
    **&lt;syntax&gt;**, **&lt;end&gt;** and **&lt;expand&gt;** node, so
    that an xpath like `command/word/*[not(@noise)]` cheaply skips them.

**orig**
:   On **&lt;subexpr&gt;**: the source-text spelling of the
    sub-expression.

**unparsed**
:   On **&lt;sql&gt;**, **&lt;json&gt;** or **&lt;javascript&gt;**:
    empty attribute marking that the contained text has not been
    further analysed.


## EXCEPTIONS

The C and Tcl parsers throw exceptions with **errorCode** matching
the following patterns when input is not syntactically valid Tcl.
The first three elements are always **PARSETCL PARSE** *subcode*; the
remaining elements give the error position and source text.

**PARSETCL** **PARSE** **MISSING_QUOTE** *idx* *text* *ofs*
:   Reached end of input (or end of word) while inside a
    `"..."`-quoted word without finding the closing quote.

**PARSETCL** **PARSE** **MISSING_CLOSE_BRACE** *idx* *text* *ofs*
:   Reached end of input while inside a `{...}`-quoted word, list
    element or variable name without finding the matching `}`.

**PARSETCL** **PARSE** **MISSING_END_ARRAY** *idx* *text* *ofs*
:   Reached end of input while inside an array index `$arr(...)`
    without finding the closing `)`.

**PARSETCL** **PARSE** **MISSING_VARIABLE_CLOSE_BRACE** *idx* *text* *ofs*
:   Reached end of input while parsing a `${name}` variable reference
    without finding the closing `}`.

**PARSETCL** **PARSE** **COMMAND_NOT_TERMINATED** *idx* *text* *ofs*
:   Inside a `[ ... ]` command substitution, ran out of input
    before finding the closing `]`.

**PARSETCL** **PARSE** **EXTRA_CHARS_AFTER_CLOSE_QUOTE** *idx* *text* *ofs*
:   Found non-whitespace, non-terminator characters after a closing
    `"` of a quoted word.

**PARSETCL** **PARSE** **LIST_ELEMENT_TRAILING_GARBAGE** *idx* *text* *ofs*
:   Inside a list, a quoted or braced element was followed by
    something other than whitespace.

**PARSETCL** **PARSE** **MISSING_OPERAND** *idx* *text* *ofs*
:   Inside an expression, an operator was followed by no operand.

**PARSETCL** **PARSE** **EXPR_BAREWORD** *idx* *text* *ofs*
:   Inside an expression, encountered an unquoted word that isn't a
    recognised function name, boolean literal or operator.

**PARSETCL** **PARSE** **EXPR_GIVEUP** *idx* *text* *ofs*
:   Inside an expression, encountered text that doesn't match any
    expected token form.

**PARSETCL** **PARSE** **NO_VARIABLE_FOUND** *idx* *text* *ofs*
:   Internal: the variable parser was invoked from the expression
    parser but produced no recognisable variable token.

**PARSETCL** **PARSE** **NO_SCRIPT_FOUND** *idx* *text* *ofs*
:   Internal: the command-substitution parser was invoked from the
    expression parser but produced no recognisable script token.

**PARSETCL** **PARSE** **EMPTY_TOKEN** *idx* *text* *ofs*
:   Internal: the expression parser refused to emit a zero-length
    token.  Indicates a parser bug if seen.

In every case, *idx* is the byte offset within *text* at which
parsing failed, *text* is the source text being parsed, and *ofs*
is the absolute offset of the start of *text* within the larger
source (zero for top-level **parsetree** / **ast** calls).


## EXAMPLES

Walk every literal command name in a script:

~~~tcl
package require parsetcl

set tree [parsetcl parsetree {
    proc greet name { puts "hello, $name" }
    greet world
}]

foreach cmd [parsetcl::xpath $tree {.//command[@name]}] {
    puts "command at line [domNode $cmd getAttribute line]: \
            [domNode $cmd getAttribute name]"
}
~~~

Find every static literal string passed to **puts** (useful for
catching accidentally-localisable user-facing text):

~~~tcl
foreach word [parsetcl::xpath [parsetcl ast $script] {
    .//command[@name='puts']/word[last()][@value]
}] {
    puts "Literal puts at idx [$word @idx]: [$word @value]"
}
~~~

Drive **subparse** to recursively analyse condition expressions and
nested scripts inside an **if**:

~~~tcl
set tree [parsetcl parsetree $script]

foreach if_cmd [parsetcl::xpath $tree {.//command[@name='if']}] {
    set words [parsetcl::xpath $if_cmd {word[position() > 1]}]

    # Second word is the condition expression
    parsetcl subparse expr [lindex $words 0]

    # Words at positions matching {body, elseif body, ..., else body}
    # are scripts.  Walk them and subparse each.
    set i 1
    while {$i < [llength $words]} {
        set w [lindex $words $i]
        if {[domNode $w getAttribute value] eq "elseif"} {
            parsetcl subparse expr   [lindex $words [incr i]]
            parsetcl subparse script [lindex $words [incr i]]
        } elseif {[domNode $w getAttribute value] eq "else"} {
            parsetcl subparse script [lindex $words [incr i]]
        } else {
            parsetcl subparse script $w
        }
        incr i
    }
}
~~~

Reconstitute the source from a parsetree (round-trip fidelity check):

~~~tcl
set src   "puts \"hello, world\"\nfoo bar"
set tree  [parsetcl parsetree $src]
set back  [domNode $tree asText]
expr {$src eq $back}    ;# -> 1
~~~

Map a byte offset back to a 1-based (line, char) pair using the
**linestarts** index attached to the enclosing script:

~~~tcl
proc offset_to_line_char {tree byteOfs} {
    set scripts [parsetcl::xpath $tree {.//script}]
    foreach s $scripts {
        set start [domNode $s getAttribute idx]
        set len   [domNode $s getAttribute len]
        if {$byteOfs >= $start && $byteOfs < $start + $len} {
            set lineofs    [domNode $s getAttribute lineofs]
            set linestarts [domNode $s getAttribute linestarts]
            set line  [lsearch -sorted -increasing -bisect -integer \
                    $linestarts $byteOfs]
            set lstart [lindex $linestarts $line]
            list [expr {$line + $lineofs + 1}] \
                    [expr {$byteOfs - $lstart + 1}]
        }
    }
}
~~~


## STABILITY

This package is in its 0 series.  The set of node names, attributes
and exception subcodes described here is expected to grow, and may be
renamed or restructured in backwards-incompatible ways before the
1.0 release.  In particular:

- Attribute names are not guaranteed stable.  In some places the C
  parser uses **len** while the Tcl-level **parse** parser uses
  **length**; this inconsistency is expected to be resolved.
- The **subparse** modes **sql**, **json** and **javascript** are
  placeholders and currently produce only an opaque
  **&lt;sql&gt;** / **&lt;json&gt;** / **&lt;javascript&gt;** node
  with the unparsed source.
- A C-level stubs API is not yet exposed.

**USING THE 0 SERIES VERSIONS IN PRODUCTION CODE IS NOT RECOMMENDED.**


## BUILDING

Besides Tcl, this package requires **tDOM** (>=0.9.6.4).  Meson finds
**tDOM** via its pkg-config file - set **PKG\_CONFIG\_PATH** to point
meson at it (and Tcl) if they're installed in a nonstandard location.
If pkg-config can't find it, the build falls back to building **tDOM**
from a meson subproject.

Tcl 9.0 is the primary supported target; building against Tcl 8.7 is
also supported.  Tcl 8.6 may work via the bundled TIP-445 shim but is
not regularly tested.

### From a Release Tarball

Download and extract [the release](https://github.com/cyanogilvie/@PACKAGE_NAME@/releases/download/v@PACKAGE_VERSION@/@PACKAGE_NAME@-@PACKAGE_VERSION@.tar.xz),
then build with meson:

```sh
wget https://github.com/cyanogilvie/@PACKAGE_NAME@/releases/download/v@PACKAGE_VERSION@/@PACKAGE_NAME@-@PACKAGE_VERSION@.tar.xz
tar xf @PACKAGE_NAME@-@PACKAGE_VERSION@.tar.xz
cd @PACKAGE_NAME@-@PACKAGE_VERSION@

meson setup builddir --buildtype=release
meson install -C builddir
```

### From the Git Sources

Fetch [the code](https://github.com/cyanogilvie/@PACKAGE_NAME@) and
submodules recursively, then build with meson:

```sh
git clone --recurse-submodules https://github.com/cyanogilvie/@PACKAGE_NAME@
cd @PACKAGE_NAME@

meson setup builddir --buildtype=release
meson install -C builddir
```

### Testing

```sh
meson test -C builddir
```

To run under valgrind (requires that Tcl and this package are built
with suitable memory debugging flags:

```sh
meson test -C builddir --wrapper='valgrind --keep-debuginfo=yes --track-origins=yes --trace-children=yes'
```


## CONFORMING TO

Aims to accept exactly the language accepted by the Tcl 9 core parser,
including command substitution, variable substitution, backslash
substitution, `{*}` word expansion, line continuation and the
canonical list / expression / subst sub-grammars.


